WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
SBOM generation belongs close to the build and release process.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:24.280
For C++ systems, that may involve CMake-style builds, package managers, vendored dependencies, generated code, toolchain versions, static linking, shared library packaging, debug and release variants, platform-specific builds, installers, archives, and container or package outputs.

3
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:30.060
The closer generation is to the artifact, the easier it is to describe what actually ships.

4
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.160
That placement also makes SBOM changes visible in normal release review.

5
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.560
A source repository SBOM and a release artifact SBOM can be different.

6
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:46.060
The repository may include tests, examples, scripts, optional features, and development tools.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:57.760
The release package may include runtime libraries, plugins, documentation, third-party binaries, platform packages, or generated files that are not obvious from source metadata.

8
00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:02.180
Neither scope is automatically wrong, but each must be named clearly.

9
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.760
Teams may maintain more than one SBOM when different audiences need different answers.

10
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.900
Naming the artifact prevents confusion during response work.

11
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:16.260
Static linking and native packaging can reduce visibility if the SBOM only lists standalone files.

12
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:25.080
A final binary may contain code from several libraries, while a shared-library package may expose runtime dependencies that are not part of the source tree.

13
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:32.220
Debug and release builds may also differ because logging, assertions, optimization, symbols, and optional components can change.

14
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.000
Platform-specific installers can add another layer of variation.

15
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.520
Release discipline should attach the SBOM to a specific versioned output.

16
00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:49.500
Store the SBOM with the release record, identify the artifact it describes, retain the tool version or generation method, and regenerate when the artifact changes.

17
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:57.840
This helps teams answer later questions about what was shipped, which components were present, and what update or license obligations applied to that release.

18
00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:05.460
It also supports rollback analysis when teams need to compare releases and explain what changed to stakeholders during response work.
